                version of linear algebra (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transform"}> integral transform
                ” = “higher matrix multiplication”!)
            %li
              %p
                such that interesting classes of
                %span.newWikiWord
                  ∞-model
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-model"} ?
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended TQFTs
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_P
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} generalized spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %ul
                %li
                  in that the “higher linear map”
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Z
                          %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_P(\Sigma)
                  assigned by the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} QFT
                  to a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi Σ
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↗
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↖
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi Σ
                                  %mi in
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi Σ
                                  %mi out
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ &amp;&amp; \Sigma \\ &amp; \nearrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow \\ \Sigma_{in} &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \Sigma_{out}}
                  is given by the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                  %mi Σ
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi P
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo ↙
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo ↘
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Σ
                                    %mi in
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi P
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Σ
                                    %mi out
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi P
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ &amp;&amp; [\Sigma,P] \\ &amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow \\ [\Sigma_{in},P] &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; [\Sigma_{out}, P]}
          %p This clearly requires that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                we fix a good ambient context of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} higher sheaf- and category theory
            %li
              %p
                and inside that a good general concept of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
          %p
            One point made by the
            = succeed "/Francis/Nadler" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"} Ben-Zvi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            in which a good notion of
            %em
              geometric
              = succeed "-function" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            can be set up nicely is the context assembled and developed in the PhD thesis of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"}> Higher Topos Theory
                – the theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
              %ul
                %li
                  %p this assembles and develops
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        work by
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
                        on
                        = succeed ";" do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
                    %li
                      %p
                        work by Simpson, Rezk, Toën, Vezzosi and others on
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categories
                        and
                        = succeed ";" do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} higher stacks
                    %li
                      %p
                        work by
                        = succeed "," do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Brown
                        = succeed "," do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Joyal
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} models
                        for
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
                        in terms of a
                        = succeed "." do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stable+Infinity-Categories"}> Stable Higher Category Theory
                – essentially the theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+and+abelian+categories"} additive and abelian categories
                lifted to the
                = succeed "-context;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %ul
                %li
                  which puts the large body of work on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                  into the above
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categorical
                  context, in essence identifying the abstract idea modeled by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-category"} pretriangulated dg-categories
                  and
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E-categories"} categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"}> Higher Algebra
                – the theory of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                = succeed "-objects" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}> symmetric
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} monoidal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
              %ul
                %li
                  %p expanding on work by Toën-Vezzosi;
                %li
                  %p which puts into its natural higher categorical context
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        the “brave new algebra” of
                        = succeed ";" do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} ring spectra
                    %li
                      %p
                        the notions of things like
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A%E2%88%9E-rings"} A∞-rings
                        and
                        = succeed "." do
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} E∞-category
          %p
            Especially for the newcomer and non-expert it must be understood that the plethora of high-powered terminology appearing here is conceptually
            %em simplified
            and unified by their description in the higher categorical context – so you
            %em gain
            by trying to learn this stuff here first before going into the standard literature:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the concept of an
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf"} (∞,1)-sheaf
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
                is
                %em much simpler
                than its model in terms of the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            %li
              %p
                the concept of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                is
                %em much simpler
                than its 1-categorical shadow as a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            %li
              %p
                the concept of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} commutative algebra in an (∞,1)-category
                is
                %em simpler
                than the details of its realization given by a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectrum
            %li
              %p
                Moreover, the gain of
                %em
                  geometric
                  = succeed "-function" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                will be that it turns out to
                %em unify
                a wealth of concepts appearing in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
          %p
            This is the reason why we bother with geometric
            = succeed "-function" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em
              geometric
              = succeed "-function" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            carries the promise of getting close to the sought-for Lawvere-ification of quantum field theory – providing its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} natural language
          %h4#21_categories
            2.1
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-categories"} ∞-categories
            that we are dealing with here are
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"}> (∞,1)-categories
              \.
          %p There are several reasons for
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                Why (∞,1)-categories?
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Why+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories%3F"} ?
          %p in the present context, the main one being that they allow to make precise the ideas summarized in the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"}> motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
              \.
          %p
            In principle one will want to eventually understand
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
            in the context of more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            – not the least because (only) for them a working well-developed full theory exists at the moment, developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
            and further developed by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p
            This subsumes and unifies notably a wealth of more-or-less ad-hoc constructions that have been known for a bit longer. In particular the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model categories
            is realized as a way to, well, model
            = succeed "-categories:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p In particular every
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
          %p is naturally a
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}> presentation of an (∞,1)-category
              \.
          %h5#211_enhancing_triangulated_categories 2.1.1 Enhancing triangulated categories
          %p
            The aim of
            %em
              geometric
              = succeed "-function" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            is to develop a good
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %em sets of functions on spaces
          %p to
          %ul
            %li
              “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-categories of
              = succeed "-functions\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "-spaces\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty)
          %p
            essentially following the general philosophy of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
          %p
            Whatever the answer is, the collection of such
            = succeed "-functions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li abelian
          %p and
          %ul
            %li monoidal
          %p in a suitable sense.
          %p
            From various examples it has become clear that the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbb{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Vect_{\mathbb{C}}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Indeed, just as functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,\mathbb{C}]
            form a vector space, functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Vect
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, Vect_{\mathbb{C}}]
            naturally form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+space"} 2-vector space
          %p
            Such a 2-vector space is in particular an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "-functions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em abelian
            collection.
          %p
            Experience shows that the right (or at least a very good)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            is a
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> stable (∞,1)-category
              \.
          %p
            It turns out that just as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model categories
            and related
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical categories
            are best thought of as, well,
            %em models
            for
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            – and in the end really all of modern homological algebra – is best thought of as models for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-categories
          %p This concerns notably
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-category"} pretriangulated dg-categoires
          %p
            which, in turn, are already
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhanced
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated categories
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            versions thereof.
          %p
            At this point you are urged to really have a look at the entry on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            and marvel about the fact that
          %ul
            %li
              %strong>
                the definition of stable
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \.
          %p
            It’s the most obvious thing in the world. And yet, it turns out that the rather involved definitions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            follow from this simple definition when one decides to look at just the 1-categorical shadow given by the
            = succeed "." do
